{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Status: NOT A BUG — report withdrawn\n\nThis ticket was filed on a wrong root-cause analysis. Investigation under /rebar-debug proved the reported defect does not exist. No code change was made and none is warranted. The original claim and its proposed fix plan are withdrawn in full; the sections below record what was actually established, so the ticket stays useful as a negative result.\n\n## Original claim (WITHDRAWN)\n\nThat `rebar link-audit --repair` can never run because `_reconciler_in_flight` (`src/rebar/_commands/fsck_repair.py:227`) fails closed on a `ModuleNotFoundError` for `rebar_reconciler`, and that `fsck --repair` is affected identically.\n\n## Why it was wrong\n\nI grepped for the import line, observed that the same import raises `ModuleNotFoundError` in a bare interpreter, and concluded the probe could never read the lock. I never read the four lines immediately above it, which already do exactly what I was proposing to add:\n\n```\nfrom rebar._engine import engine_dir\neng = str(engine_dir())\nif eng not in sys.path:\n    sys.path.insert(0, eng)  # so the top-level rebar_reconciler package resolves\nfrom rebar_reconciler import _advisory_lock as advisory\n```\n\nThe function resolves the engine directory before importing. Re-tested exactly as the function does it: `engine_dir()` resolves and exists, the import succeeds, and `check_pass_lock` returns a real answer. I asserted a mechanism from a partial read instead of observing it run.\n\n## What was actually proven\n\nSampled `refs/reconciler/lock` every 10s for two minutes. The blob was byte-identical across all 12 samples — same oid `6e5b4468`, heartbeat frozen at `1785386477382441825` (04:41:17), `lease_secs` 120. A frozen heartbeat means the holder was not renewing, so the lease expired at 04:43:17.\n\nDiscriminating experiment, run at lease age 220s:\n\n- H1 — `check_pass_lock` honors lease expiry → predicted `False`.\n- H2 — it returns `True` whenever the ref exists, so a stale lock blocks repair forever → predicted `True`.\n\nResult: `check_pass_lock` returned `False`. **H1 confirmed, H2 rejected.**\n\nThe guard is correct in both directions: it reports held while the lease is live and free once it expires. Every earlier refusal coincided with a genuinely live lease — the first attempt hit one at 117s of a 120s lease. There is no live-lock, no stale-holder defect, and no always-held condition. The correct action was to wait out the reconciler pass, which is precisely what the guard exists to enforce.\n\n`defect_legitimacy`: **intended-behavior**. Authoritative source: the function's own documented fail-closed contract at `src/rebar/_commands/fsck_repair.py:227-234`, whose behaviour the experiment matched exactly.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [x] The reported defect is disproven by a discriminating runtime experiment, with the result recorded on this ticket; verified by the H1/H2 experiment above.\n- [x] No code change is made and no fix is landed, per the not-a-bug guard; verified by this ticket recording no file impact and no referencing commit.\n- [x] The withdrawn claim about `fsck --repair` is retracted in writing; verified by the \"Original claim (WITHDRAWN)\" section above.\n\n## Follow-up (not this ticket)\n\n`_reconciler_in_flight` has no test coverage — `grep -rn _reconciler_in_flight tests/` returns nothing. That is a genuine coverage gap but not a defect, and not what this ticket claimed."}},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1785387783936382001,"uuid":"02888ab7-578d-4b1f-91b6-aebb34de4b01"}